@inherits LayoutComponentBase

@* The display area's shell (TECHNICAL_SPECIFICATION §11.5). Deliberately NOT MainLayout: that layout
   renders a session header with the signed-in name, a Security link, and a sign-out form, none of which
   mean anything to a tablet bolted to a table — and its <AuthorizeView> would happily present a display
   device as though it were a person. A display has no session to manage and no navigation to offer; it
   has one job, which is to be readable from across a table.

   Full-bleed and centred, with the type scaled off the viewport so the same markup is legible on a 7"
   tablet and on a spare monitor. Styles are inline for the same reason every other page here keeps them
   inline: App.razor links the static stylesheet, so component CSS isolation is not active.

   It does carry the wall clock (§11.7), which is the one piece of shared chrome that means something
   here: a display is the screen a whole table looks at, and it is the natural place for the restaurant's
   own time to live. The card therefore centres inside a column rather than inside the shell itself.

   It carries the colophon (§11.9) too, quieter than elsewhere. A tablet is arguably not a "user
   interacting with it remotely" in AGPL §13's sense, so a carve-out here would be defensible — but it
   would mean the one screen a whole restaurant looks at is the only one that does not say what it is
   running, which is a strange place to draw that line for one line of small type. *@

<div class="display-shell">
    <div class="display-main">
        @Body
    </div>

    <RestaurantClockFooter />
    <AppColophon />
</div>

<style>
    .display-shell {
        min-height: 100dvh;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        background: var(--surface, #f7f8fa);
    }

    .display-main {
        flex: 1 1 auto;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        padding: clamp(0.75rem, 2.5vw, 2rem);
    }

    /* Bigger than the app footer's: this one is read from across a table, not from a hand. */
    .display-shell .app-footer {
        background: transparent;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline, #e2e6ec);
    }

    .display-shell .restaurant-clock {
        font-size: clamp(0.85rem, 2.2vw, 1.05rem);
    }

    /* Smaller than elsewhere, not larger. The clock above it is meant to be read from across a table;
       the colophon is meant to be legible to somebody who has walked up to the screen to find out
       what it is. Scaling it with the viewport like the clock would put a licence notice in the same
       type size as the time. */
    .display-shell .app-colophon {
        background: transparent;
        font-size: 0.7rem;
    }

    .display-card {
        width: 100%;
        max-width: 44rem;
        background: var(--surface-raised, #ffffff);
        border: 1px solid var(--hairline, #e2e6ec);
        border-radius: 1rem;
        padding: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 2rem);
        text-align: center;
    }

    .display-card h1 {
        margin: 0.25rem 0 0.5rem;
        font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 6vw, 3rem);
        line-height: 1.1;
        letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    }

    .display-eyebrow {
        margin: 0;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 0.08em;
        font-size: 0.75rem;
        font-weight: 700;
        color: var(--ink-soft, #55636f);
    }

    .display-lede {
        margin: 0.5rem auto 0;
        max-width: 30rem;
        color: var(--ink-soft, #55636f);
    }
</style>
